rians, Monks).","color":"#c084fc","family":"Psicodelia, garage y shoegaze","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#british-invasion"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":11208940655875,"artist":"Petula Clark","artist_id":75462260226532,"title":"Downtown","year":1964,"ess":0,"yt":"Zx06XNfDvk0","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"El diluvio: invasión británica y garage","scene_id":245539326529376,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#british-invasion","chapters":["Sección 2.5"],"pages":[[1,23,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":23,"text":"…music, forcing even the most conservative teen-idols to adopt a more lively style. Dusty Springfield's You Don't Have To Say You Love Me (1963) and Petula Clark's Downtown (1964) are truly \"youthful\". Britain was not only the Beatles. In fact, the Beatles were mis-representing the British scene. The three…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":199,"text":"…fusion of hypnotic disco beats, catchy melodies, surreal arrangements, and Sixties-style naif enthusiasm. Mixing Byrds with Abba, and Hendrix with Petula Clark, and James Brown and the Mamas & Papas, songs such as I Wanna Be Adored, She Bangs The Drums and Made Of Stone bridged different languages and…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":270,"text":"…(2) inherited the Primitives' passion for melodious simplicity. Fronted by former Talulah Gosh's singer Amelia Fletcher, they resurrected the age of Petula Clark, the girl-groups and bubblegum music on Heavenly Vs Satan (1991). Their romantic and naive approach to the pop tune evolved with Le Jardin De…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"British Invasion Era Artist","body":"Petula Clark was a notable artist from the 1960s British Invasion. \"Having walked through 